  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 345 AM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nWednesday's storm system will be gone as quickly as it moves in, \nwith transient ridging expected to build back into the area on \nThursday. Areas west of the Laramie range should easily warm 10F \nabove Wednesday's values, whereas those to the east may be similar \nto just a few degrees warmer thanks to cool southeast surface winds \nand cloud cover increasing once again. Attention then shifts to the \nnext shortwave trough moving in from the Pacific, which will start \nto influence our weather Thursday night. Ensembles are now in \nexcellent agreement depicting this storm system as a largely \nlimited precipitation event, mostly confined to the higher \nterrain. Nearly all ensemble members have little to no \nprecipitation falling over the High Plains thanks to the upper \nlevel low remaining north of the area. The flow aloft remaining \nsouthwest Thursday night and much of Friday will also be an \nunfavorable direction for orographic lift for most of the area \n(except Carbon county). Spotty moisture in the cloud layer \nshould support on and off snow showers beginning Thursday night \nand continuing into Friday in Carbon County. While some brief \nmixing with rain is possible at the onset, this event is \nexpected to be primarily snow. Advisory criteria snowfall \namounts are possible in our western areas. The Sierra Madre \nrange has the highest probability (around 60%) for Advisory \ncriteria snowfall, while the Snowy range falls around 30%, and \nthe valleys of Carbon county are around 20%. \n\nPrecipitation chances will be slim further east, but we can still \nexpect this system to bring a change in the weather as a cold front \npasses through the High Plains Friday night. While Carbon, Albany, \nand Converse counties will be noticeably colder Friday, remaining \nareas of the High Plains should still manage to reach near or \nslightly above average high temperatures. Saturday, meanwhile, is \nexpected to be about 5F colder than average across the board. Expect \nlocally breezy to windy conditions, particularly on Friday and \nFriday night, but the probability for high winds is low at this time \n(about 20 to 30% for the wind prone areas). While ridging will try \nto recover behind this system, ensembles are in relatively good\nagreement on limiting the warm-up Sunday and Monday, with \ntemperatures remaining generally near or even slightly below \nseasonal averages.",
